cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "Syntonic Engine: gains from measured facts, no tuning"
abstract: |-
  Every law in this package computes its gains from statistics measured in the signal and a few facts about the machine measured once, never from tuning. The syntonic engine sets the time window of a correction or an estimate by a law derived from first principles (Theorem U' of the Syntony Principle); it decides when to correct. Since 0.3.0, two laws in the Python package also set how much:

  - Syntonic loop law: a servo joint's PI gains, recomputed every 0.5 s as those that minimise the loop error predicted from the disturbance statistics measured live, among the gains that keep the loop robust. Its facts: the loop timing, two data-sheet values, and the servo's delay measured once at commissioning.
  - Riccati attitude law: a one-axis attitude estimator with one gyro bias per throttle level; its gains are the Riccati gains of the model that the measured noise and drift describe.

  Results (simulation, pre-registered, fresh seeds):
  - Servo: on a realistic MAESTRO 1 bench (1 kHz loop, 333 Hz servo frames, PWM latency, compliance, gear play, quantization), the loop law is at parity with or better than a PID tuned at commissioning with the same lead in all nine circumstances (worst ratio 1.009; 0.78 under a tenfold noise step), and better than the conventional tuned PID in all nine.
  - Flight estimation: on a one-axis bench replaying the X500's logged noise, the Riccati attitude law has a lower error than three Kalman filters tuned against truth in all six circumstances (ratios 0.27 to 0.99). This bench (block-bootstrap replay, unknown initial attitude) was defined after a first confirmation on a looped bench failed; that failure is kept on record.
  - Of 14 pre-registered predictions, 3 failed.

  Limits: simulation only; one flight axis with the plant at rest; three short records from one airframe; law structures chosen on development seeds; rivals tuned against truth, an oracle no real filter has. The new laws are Python only; the engine itself is unchanged in Python, C++ and Rust. The deciding test is a real log of 60 s or more at half throttle.

  This version contains all of 0.2.9: the 0.2.9 release was created before its branch was merged, so its archive holds the 0.2.8 code.

  Details: bench/goal_2026-11-02/NOTE.md in the repository.
